From: Jahshaka <jahshaka@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Vulkan: fill the empty GPU sample-profiling hooks with timestamp queries

WHY (Jahshaka lane MON-P1c, 2026-09-12; SPECS/RENDER_LOOP_MONITOR_SPEC.md D3)

At this pin `VulkanRenderSystem::initGPUProfiling`, `deinitGPUProfiling`,
`beginGPUSampleProfile` and `endGPUSampleProfile` are EMPTY BODIES
(OgreVulkanRenderSystem.cpp:2665-2671). There is therefore no GPU time anywhere
in the Vulkan render system: not per pass, not per workspace, not per frame.
The D3D11 and Metal render systems implement the same hooks; Vulkan does not.

Jahshaka's render-loop monitor is a data collector for engine reviews, and the
owner's words on 2026-09-12 were "we want GPU more than CPU, but we need both".
Without this patch every capture answers "GPU: not measured", and the one
question the owner is asking — why a simple scene on an RTX 4080 SUPER runs at
34 fps — cannot be split into CPU-bound and GPU-bound.

WHAT IT DOES

* `initGPUProfiling` checks that the device reports a non-zero
  `timestampPeriod` AND that the graphics queue family reports non-zero
  `timestampValidBits` (a device may have timestamps on some queues only), then
  creates TWO `VK_QUERY_TYPE_TIMESTAMP` pools of 4096 queries.
* `beginGPUSampleProfile` / `endGPUSampleProfile` write a TOP_OF_PIPE and a
  BOTTOM_OF_PIPE timestamp into the current pool. Samples NEST (a scene pass
  owns a shadow node whose passes run inside it), so they ride a stack, exactly
  like the caller's own.
* Results are read back TWO FRAMES LATE and NON-BLOCKING
  (`VK_QUERY_RESULT_WITH_AVAILABILITY_BIT`): a sample whose pair is not yet
  available is skipped, never waited for and never reported as zero. The CPU
  never stalls on the GPU.
* THE POOL RESET IS THE DESIGN CONSTRAINT. A timestamp WRITE is legal inside a
  render pass; `vkCmdResetQueryPool` is not. The pools therefore alternate, and
  the one about to be written is read back and reset at ONE point the host
  calls explicitly — `getCustomAttribute( "JahGpuFrameBegin" )` — at the top of
  the frame, before any workspace updates, where ending the encoders costs
  nothing because none is open.

WHY getCustomAttribute AND NOT NEW VIRTUALS

The readback needs a channel out of the render system. `getCustomAttribute` is
already the render system's escape hatch (it hands out `VkDevice`,
`VulkanQueue`, ...), it adds NO OgreMain ABI surface, and — because it THROWS
on an unknown name — a build WITHOUT this patch answers the host's probe by
throwing, which is exactly how the host learns there is no GPU timing. No
version flag is needed anywhere.

Three names are added, all under the same define:
  "JahGpuTimestamps"     -> bool: are timestamps live right now
  "JahGpuFrameBegin"     -> rotate pools, read back, reset (call once a frame)
  "JahGpuSampleResults"  -> appends (sampleId, milliseconds) pairs

THE TWO OFF-SWITCHES (owner decision D3, 2026-09-12)

1. BUILD. Everything above is inside `#ifdef JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS`, set by the
   new `JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS` CMake option, which DEFAULTS OFF. Without it the
   four hooks compile to upstream's empty bodies byte for byte, the class
   carries no extra members and the binary contains no query-pool code at all.
   `build-ogre.sh` passes `-DJAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS=ON` for dev builds and
   `=OFF` when `JAH_PRODUCTION=1` (release and packaging lanes).
2. RUNTIME. Even in a dev build nothing exists until the host calls
   `initGPUProfiling`, which Jahshaka does only when a capture starts; the
   pools are destroyed when it stops. A dev build with no capture running owns
   zero query pools, which a suite asserts.

UPSTREAM-REPORTABLE: the empty Vulkan hooks are an upstream gap, not a
Jahshaka-specific need — Ogre's own `Profiler` calls them and gets nothing on
Vulkan. Recorded for SPECS/OGRE_UPSTREAM_ISSUES.md.

SCOPE: `RenderSystems/Vulkan` only. No OgreMain header, no ABI change, no
behaviour change of any kind when the option is off. SOURCE patch: every tree
must re-run `build-ogre.sh`.

Verified: `git apply --check` and `git apply --reverse --check` against the
pristine pin; built and measured on an RTX 4080 SUPER (driver 595.84) —
test_engine's monitor_gpu_timestamps drained 5 frames with 42 passes carrying
GPU times.

diff --git a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/CMakeLists.txt b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/CMakeLists.txt
index 22c552197..a9f503d7f 100644
--- a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -103,6 +103,20 @@ if( OGRE_VULKAN_USE_SWAPPY )
 	target_link_libraries(RenderSystem_Vulkan ${AndroidSwappy_LIBRARY})
 endif()
 
+# --- Jahshaka local patch 0027: GPU timestamp sampling ----------------------
+# OFF BY DEFAULT, and that is lock 1 of the two the owner asked for (D3,
+# 2026-09-12): without this option the three GPU-profiling hooks compile to
+# upstream's empty stubs byte for byte, the render system carries no extra
+# members and no query pool type exists anywhere in the binary. A production or
+# packaging build simply does not pass it. `build-ogre.sh` passes it for DEV
+# builds (i.e. unless JAH_PRODUCTION=1). Lock 2 is at runtime: even with this
+# on, no pool is created until a capture starts.
+option(JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS "Jahshaka: fill Vulkan's GPU sample-profiling hooks with timestamp queries (dev builds only)" OFF)
+if( JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS )
+	target_compile_definitions(RenderSystem_Vulkan PRIVATE JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS)
+endif()
+# --- end Jahshaka local patch ----------------------------------------------
+
 if (OGRE_CONFIG_THREADS)
   target_link_libraries(RenderSystem_Vulkan ${OGRE_THREAD_LIBRARIES})
 endif ()
diff --git a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/include/OgreVulkanRenderSystem.h b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/include/OgreVulkanRenderSystem.h
index cf8dce196..5dbf18180 100644
--- a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/include/OgreVulkanRenderSystem.h
+++ b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/include/OgreVulkanRenderSystem.h
@@ -149,6 +149,47 @@ namespace Ogre
         void destroyVkResources0();
         void destroyVkResources1();
 
+#ifdef JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS
+        // ---- JAHSHAKA patch 0027: GPU timestamp sampling ------------------
+        //
+        // Upstream leaves initGPUProfiling / beginGPUSampleProfile /
+        // endGPUSampleProfile as empty stubs on Vulkan, so there is no GPU time
+        // anywhere in this render system. Jahshaka's render-loop monitor needs
+        // it ("we want GPU more than CPU, but we need both" — owner, 2026-09-12)
+        // and fills them with plain timestamp queries.
+        //
+        // ENTIRELY BEHIND THIS DEFINE. Without JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS the class has
+        // no extra members and the three hooks compile to upstream's empty
+        // bodies, byte for byte — which is what a production or packaging build
+        // gets (owner decision D3, lock 1 of 2).
+        //
+        // TWO POOLS, READ TWO FRAMES LATE. A timestamp write is legal inside a
+        // render pass; a query-pool RESET is not. So the pool for frame N is
+        // reset at the START of frame N, outside every encoder, at the one
+        // moment the host calls in (the "JahGpuFrameBegin" custom attribute) —
+        // and the pool being recycled then is the one written two frames ago,
+        // whose results are read back non-blocking with the availability bit
+        // just before it is reset. Nothing ever stalls the CPU on the GPU.
+        static const uint32 kJahMaxGpuQueries = 4096u;
+        struct JahGpuSample
+        {
+            uint32 hash;   ///< the caller's id for this sample (hashCache)
+            uint32 query;  ///< the begin query index; end is query + 1
+        };
+        VkQueryPool               mJahQueryPool[2];
+        bool                      mJahPoolWritten[2];
+        std::vector<JahGpuSample> mJahSamples[2];
+        std::vector<JahGpuSample> mJahSampleStack;   ///< samples nest
+        std::vector<std::pair<uint32, float> > mJahResults;
+        uint32                    mJahPoolIdx;
+        uint32                    mJahNextQuery;
+        float                     mJahTimestampPeriodNs;
+        bool                      mJahGpuProfiling;
+        /// Rotates the pools, reads back the one being recycled and resets it.
+        /// Called through getCustomAttribute( "JahGpuFrameBegin" ).
+        void jahGpuFrameBegin();
+#endif
+
     public:
         VulkanRenderSystem( const NameValuePairList *options );
         ~VulkanRenderSystem() override;
diff --git a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanRenderSystem.cpp b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanRenderSystem.cpp
index 06e0c4728..e15065cd7 100644
--- a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanRenderSystem.cpp
+++ b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/OgreVulkanRenderSystem.cpp
@@ -223,6 +223,12 @@ namespace Ogre
         mCurrentAutoParamsBufferPtr( 0 ),
         mCurrentAutoParamsBufferSpaceLeft( 0 ),
         mDevice( 0 ),
+#ifdef JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS
+        mJahPoolIdx( 0u ),
+        mJahNextQuery( 0u ),
+        mJahTimestampPeriodNs( 0.0f ),
+        mJahGpuProfiling( false ),
+#endif
         mCache( 0 ),
         mPso( 0 ),
         mComputePso( 0 ),
@@ -2662,13 +2668,177 @@ namespace Ogre
 #endif
     }
     //-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-    void VulkanRenderSystem::initGPUProfiling() {}
+    // JAHSHAKA patch 0027: the GPU timestamp half of these hooks. Upstream
+    // leaves all four empty on Vulkan, so nothing in this render system can say
+    // what the GPU spent on a pass. See OgreVulkanRenderSystem.h for the design
+    // and for why every line of it is behind JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS.
     //-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-    void VulkanRenderSystem::deinitGPUProfiling() {}
+    void VulkanRenderSystem::initGPUProfiling()
+    {
+#ifdef JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS
+        if( mJahGpuProfiling || !mDevice )
+            return;
+
+        VkPhysicalDeviceProperties props;
+        vkGetPhysicalDeviceProperties( mDevice->mPhysicalDevice, &props );
+        // timestampPeriod == 0 means the device cannot do timestamps at all;
+        // timestampValidBits == 0 on the queue family means this queue cannot.
+        if( props.limits.timestampPeriod <= 0.0f )
+            return;
+        uint32 numFamilies = 0u;
+        vkGetPhysicalDeviceQueueFamilyProperties( mDevice->mPhysicalDevice, &numFamilies, 0 );
+        FastArray<VkQueueFamilyProperties> families;
+        families.resize( numFamilies );
+        vkGetPhysicalDeviceQueueFamilyProperties( mDevice->mPhysicalDevice, &numFamilies,
+                                                  families.begin() );
+        const uint32 familyIdx = mDevice->mGraphicsQueue.getFamilyIdx();
+        if( familyIdx >= numFamilies || families[familyIdx].timestampValidBits == 0u )
+            return;
+
+        VkQueryPoolCreateInfo poolCi;
+        makeVkStruct( poolCi, VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_QUERY_POOL_CREATE_INFO );
+        poolCi.queryType = VK_QUERY_TYPE_TIMESTAMP;
+        poolCi.queryCount = kJahMaxGpuQueries;
+        for( size_t i = 0u; i < 2u; ++i )
+        {
+            mJahQueryPool[i] = 0;
+            mJahPoolWritten[i] = false;
+            mJahSamples[i].clear();
+            if( vkCreateQueryPool( mDevice->mDevice, &poolCi, 0, &mJahQueryPool[i] ) != VK_SUCCESS )
+            {
+                for( size_t j = 0u; j < i; ++j )
+                    vkDestroyQueryPool( mDevice->mDevice, mJahQueryPool[j], 0 );
+                return;
+            }
+        }
+        mJahTimestampPeriodNs = props.limits.timestampPeriod;
+        mJahPoolIdx = 0u;
+        mJahNextQuery = 0u;
+        mJahSampleStack.clear();
+        mJahResults.clear();
+        mJahGpuProfiling = true;
+#endif
+    }
+    //-------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    void VulkanRenderSystem::deinitGPUProfiling()
+    {
+#ifdef JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS
+        if( !mJahGpuProfiling )
+            return;
+        mJahGpuProfiling = false;
+        // The pools may still be referenced by command buffers in flight.
+        mDevice->stall();
+        for( size_t i = 0u; i < 2u; ++i )
+        {
+            if( mJahQueryPool[i] )
+                vkDestroyQueryPool( mDevice->mDevice, mJahQueryPool[i], 0 );
+            mJahQueryPool[i] = 0;
+            mJahPoolWritten[i] = false;
+            mJahSamples[i].clear();
+        }
+        mJahSampleStack.clear();
+        mJahResults.clear();
+#endif
+    }
+    //-------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    void VulkanRenderSystem::beginGPUSampleProfile( const String &name, uint32 *hashCache )
+    {
+#ifdef JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS
+        if( !mJahGpuProfiling )
+            return;
+        // Two queries per sample (begin, end). Out of room: the sample is
+        // simply not recorded, and the caller reports "no GPU time" for it
+        // rather than a wrong number.
+        if( mJahNextQuery + 2u > kJahMaxGpuQueries )
+            return;
+        JahGpuSample sample;
+        sample.hash = hashCache ? *hashCache : 0u;
+        sample.query = mJahNextQuery;
+        mJahNextQuery += 2u;
+        // A timestamp write is legal inside a render pass, which is where most
+        // of these land.
+        vkCmdWriteTimestamp( mDevice->mGraphicsQueue.getCurrentCmdBuffer(),
+                             VK_PIPELINE_STAGE_TOP_OF_PIPE_BIT, mJahQueryPool[mJahPoolIdx],
+                             sample.query );
+        mJahPoolWritten[mJahPoolIdx] = true;
+        mJahSampleStack.push_back( sample );
+#else
+        (void)name;
+        (void)hashCache;
+#endif
+    }
     //-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-    void VulkanRenderSystem::beginGPUSampleProfile( const String &name, uint32 *hashCache ) {}
+    void VulkanRenderSystem::endGPUSampleProfile( const String &name )
+    {
+#ifdef JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS
+        if( !mJahGpuProfiling || mJahSampleStack.empty() )
+            return;
+        const JahGpuSample sample = mJahSampleStack.back();
+        mJahSampleStack.pop_back();
+        vkCmdWriteTimestamp( mDevice->mGraphicsQueue.getCurrentCmdBuffer(),
+                             VK_PIPELINE_STAGE_BOTTOM_OF_PIPE_BIT, mJahQueryPool[mJahPoolIdx],
+                             sample.query + 1u );
+        mJahSamples[mJahPoolIdx].push_back( sample );
+#else
+        (void)name;
+#endif
+    }
+#ifdef JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS
     //-------------------------------------------------------------------------
-    void VulkanRenderSystem::endGPUSampleProfile( const String &name ) {}
+    void VulkanRenderSystem::jahGpuFrameBegin()
+    {
+        if( !mJahGpuProfiling )
+            return;
+        // Any sample left open by a frame that threw would corrupt the next
+        // one's nesting.
+        mJahSampleStack.clear();
+        mJahPoolIdx = ( mJahPoolIdx + 1u ) & 1u;
+        mJahResults.clear();
+
+        const uint32 written = (uint32)mJahSamples[mJahPoolIdx].size();
+        if( mJahPoolWritten[mJahPoolIdx] && written > 0u )
+        {
+            // NON-BLOCKING. WITH_AVAILABILITY gives a third word per query that
+            // is zero while the GPU has not written it; a sample whose pair is
+            // not both available is skipped, never waited for and never
+            // reported as zero.
+            FastArray<uint64> raw;
+            raw.resize( (size_t)kJahMaxGpuQueries * 2u );
+            uint32 highest = 0u;
+            for( size_t i = 0u; i < mJahSamples[mJahPoolIdx].size(); ++i )
+                highest = std::max( highest, mJahSamples[mJahPoolIdx][i].query + 2u );
+            const VkResult res = vkGetQueryPoolResults(
+                mDevice->mDevice, mJahQueryPool[mJahPoolIdx], 0u, highest,
+                (size_t)highest * 2u * sizeof( uint64 ), raw.begin(), 2u * sizeof( uint64 ),
+                VK_QUERY_RESULT_64_BIT | VK_QUERY_RESULT_WITH_AVAILABILITY_BIT );
+            if( res == VK_SUCCESS || res == VK_NOT_READY )
+            {
+                for( size_t i = 0u; i < mJahSamples[mJahPoolIdx].size(); ++i )
+                {
+                    const JahGpuSample &sample = mJahSamples[mJahPoolIdx][i];
+                    const uint64 *b = raw.begin() + (size_t)sample.query * 2u;
+                    const uint64 *e = raw.begin() + (size_t)( sample.query + 1u ) * 2u;
+                    if( b[1] == 0u || e[1] == 0u || e[0] < b[0] )
+                        continue;   // not back yet, or a wrapped counter
+                    const float ms = float( double( e[0] - b[0] ) *
+                                            double( mJahTimestampPeriodNs ) * 1e-6 );
+                    mJahResults.push_back( std::pair<uint32, float>( sample.hash, ms ) );
+                }
+            }
+        }
+        mJahSamples[mJahPoolIdx].clear();
+
+        // THE RESET MUST BE OUTSIDE EVERY ENCODER (a render pass cannot contain
+        // vkCmdResetQueryPool). This is the one call the host makes at the top
+        // of the frame, before any workspace updates, so ending the encoders
+        // here costs nothing: there is nothing open yet in a normal frame.
+        mDevice->mGraphicsQueue.endAllEncoders();
+        vkCmdResetQueryPool( mDevice->mGraphicsQueue.getCurrentCmdBuffer(),
+                             mJahQueryPool[mJahPoolIdx], 0u, kJahMaxGpuQueries );
+        mJahPoolWritten[mJahPoolIdx] = false;
+        mJahNextQuery = 0u;
+    }
+#endif
     //-------------------------------------------------------------------------
     void VulkanRenderSystem::endGpuDebuggerFrameCapture( Window *window, const bool bDiscard )
     {
@@ -2709,6 +2879,36 @@ namespace Ogre
             *(VulkanQueue **)pData = &mDevice->mGraphicsQueue;
             return;
         }
+#ifdef JAH_GPU_TIMESTAMPS
+        // JAHSHAKA patch 0027 — the readback channel for the GPU timestamps
+        // above. getCustomAttribute is used rather than new virtuals so the
+        // patch adds NO OgreMain ABI surface, and the names simply do not exist
+        // in a build without the define: the host asks for "JahGpuTimestamps",
+        // catches the exception, and reports gpuCompiled = false.
+        else if( name == "JahGpuTimestamps" )
+        {
+            // "was this render system built with timestamp support, and did the
+            // device accept it" — the runtime half of the answer is whether a
+            // pool exists, i.e. whether initGPUProfiling succeeded.
+            *(bool *)pData = mJahGpuProfiling;
+            return;
+        }
+        else if( name == "JahGpuFrameBegin" )
+        {
+            jahGpuFrameBegin();
+            return;
+        }
+        else if( name == "JahGpuSampleResults" )
+        {
+            // (sample id, milliseconds) for the frame recorded two frames ago.
+            // Appended, never cleared, so one caller can drain several.
+            std::vector<std::pair<uint32, float> > *out =
+                (std::vector<std::pair<uint32, float> > *)pData;
+            out->insert( out->end(), mJahResults.begin(), mJahResults.end() );
+            mJahResults.clear();
+            return;
+        }
+#endif
 
         OGRE_EXCEPT( Exception::ERR_INVALIDPARAMS, "Attribute not found: " + name,
                      "VulkanRenderSystem::getCustomAttribute" );
